# Vault re-entry context card — shared contract (converged 8/12)

Converged design from the Alyosha + Hollow email round on "how should the vault serve
memory/retrieval" (thread: "Memory/retrieval round — shared vault contract vs
agent-specific retrieval"). Avi steered the pilot target to the AIOS current-state /
workboard re-entry layer. Nothing here was implemented — Avi must give the go.

## Shared finding
The vault is already the canonical durable memory. The missing layer is NOT another
body of knowledge — it is a small, predictable representation of LIVE project state.
Both Hermes (Alyosha) and OpenClaw (Hollow) pay a re-derivation cost because current
state is scattered across files.

## The contract — a project context card
- Sufficient for orientation once the project is known.
- NOT itself the final evidence (points to authoritative source, doesn't replace it).
- Small enough to maintain during real work.
- Linked to the authoritative source(s).
- Refreshed on MATERIAL STATE CHANGE; session close is only the final checkpoint
  (Hollow's state-change rule is better than a session-end ritual — sessions get
  forked/interrupted and a remembered sign-off ritual drifts).

## Minimum fields
1. Project / purpose
2. Current state
3. Next meaningful action
4. Boundaries / non-authorizations
5. Read next / authoritative source(s)
6. Freshness: last material update; updated/verified by; basis for that verification
7. Optional one-line "what changed" when not obvious from current state

## Freshness balance (the right weight)
- Writer verifies the update at material change.
- Arriving agent TRUSTS the card for orientation; follows the linked source only when
  the question needs evidence, the card is ambiguous, or there's reason to suspect
  intervening change.
- Do NOT require every arriving agent to re-verify against source — that recreates the
  full re-derivation cost under a new name.

## Scope boundaries (all agreed)
- START_HERE = router; global workboard = portfolio view. Neither is a mandatory
  re-read once routed to the correct card.
- Inbox resurfacing is a separate DISCOVERY practice — test alongside, not part of the
  card contract.
- Semantic retrieval (neither agent has semantic VAULT retrieval; both have it for own
  transcripts) is platform-specific and separate.
- No global workboard rewrite, no Inbox change, no semantic indexing, no new memory
  provider, no integration, no mass reorg, no access expansion.
- No duplication of project history into the card.

## Pilot (AIOS current-state re-entry layer — Avi's steer)
Why not SPED command center: SPED is mostly Hollow's lane (weak test of a SHARED
contract); the AIOS workboard/current-state is re-entered daily by Avi + both agents
and the 8/11 workboard drift was literally the cross-agent re-entry failure — tests at
the actual wound.

Pilot object — a card answering only:
- What is the AIOS operating layer currently trying to accomplish?
- Which 3–5 lanes are genuinely active now?
- For each active lane: next meaningful action + governing boundary.
- Which authoritative project/card/source to open next.
- When and by whom this re-entry state was last materially updated.

NOT its job: preserve history, full project status, duplicate every workboard lane,
replace project context cards/evidence, become an Inbox/task manager, or coexist
silently with an overlapping old "current" artifact.

**Authority relationship (critical, resolve before first file):** the pilot card must
NOT become a second workboard beside the existing one. Option A (endorsed): existing
workboard stays FROZEN baseline/history during the test; experimental card is visibly
labeled the SOLE current re-entry artifact for the duration. Option B: reduce the
existing workboard through the pilot format (destroys the baseline). Pick A — rollback
trivial, honest before/after.

**Pass condition added (8/12):** at no point may two artifacts plausibly claim to be
the live AIOS state without an explicit authority label. If unavoidable, pilot fails
before content quality matters.

## Test sequence
1. Baseline: cold-read existing workboard; record active state/next/boundary/source,
   disagreements, hunting, stale lanes, what Avi must correct.
2. Define minimal card standard.
3. Create one experimental card from canonical evidence at one predictable path.
4. Avi + both agents independently read it; report the same four outputs.
5. Exercise through several real AIOS state changes over a short window (not one event).
6. Responsible agent refreshes on material change WITHOUT Avi synchronizing.
7. Compare accuracy, maintenance burden, re-entry effort, disagreement vs baseline.
8. Stop. Avi decides retire/revise/adopt. No propagation elsewhere by default.

